<!--
    Four suites, deliberately separate rather than one `tests/` bag, because each answers a
    different question and each has a different cost:

      unit        pure PHP, no kernel, no database. The domain's arithmetic and invariants.
      integration real PostgreSQL. Proves what the *database* does — column fidelity, tenant scoping.
      functional  the application's HTTP surface through the kernel.
      e2e         a really-booted server, spoken to over the wire.

    Running `phpunit` with no suite filter runs all four; CI and the local gate both do that.
-->
<phpunit
    bootstrap="tests/bootstrap.php"
    cacheDirectory="var/phpunit"
    colors="true"
    failOnRisky="true"
    failOnWarning="true"
    beStrictAboutOutputDuringTests="true"
    displayDetailsOnTestsThatTriggerWarnings="true"
    displayDetailsOnTestsThatTriggerErrors="true"
    displayDetailsOnPhpunitDeprecations="true"
>
    <testsuites>
        <testsuite name="unit">
            <directory>tests/Unit</directory>
        </testsuite>
        <testsuite name="integration">
            <directory>tests/Integration</directory>
        </testsuite>
        <testsuite name="functional">
            <directory>tests/Functional</directory>
        </testsuite>
        <testsuite name="e2e">
            <directory>tests/E2E</directory>
        </testsuite>
    </testsuites>

    <source>
        <include>
            <directory>src</directory>
        </include>
    </source>

    <php>
        <ini name="error_reporting" value="-1"/>
        <!--
            Integration suite target. Overridden by the real environment in CI, and these defaults are
            throwaway local values.

            MANY roles, not one. The count lives in provision-test-database.sh, not here, because it has grown
            at four rounds now. The tenancy proof is only worth something against the topology production
            uses: a role that owns the policed tables can remove their policies or TRUNCATE them in one
            statement, so a suite provisioned with a single role certifies a connection that could step
            around the thing being certified. EVERY ROLE BEYOND THE FIRST TWO exists to make one refusal
            branch provable against a real connection rather than only through a pure predicate — a fixture
            that cannot
            express a dangerous shape cannot detect it, which is how three cross-tenant breaches survived
            into round 5. What each role is for is documented in scripts/dev/provision-test-database.sh,
            which creates them. THE SUPERUSER PAIR BELOW IS REQUIRED — it was documented here as optional
            "only grants a predefined role inside one test" until round 15, when MANY tests came to gate on it and
            four of those were the only evidence that a security fix is load-bearing. A missing or wrong
            credential now FAILS rather than skipping, exactly as an unreachable database does.
        -->
        <env name="TWES_TEST_DSN" value="pgsql:host=127.0.0.1;port=5432;dbname=twes_in_test"/>
        <!--
            THE APPLICATION'S OWN ENVIRONMENT, needed by the `functional` and `e2e` suites, which boot the real
            kernel. Declared HERE rather than in a `.env.test` file for the reason `Kernel` gives about Flex: the
            wiring is visible rather than implied, and one file lists everything a test run needs.

            KERNEL_CLASS is what `WebTestCase` resolves the kernel from. Without it the suite dies complaining the
            kernel was booted too early, which sends a reader hunting for a lifecycle bug that is not there.
        -->
        <env name="APP_ENV" value="test"/>
        <!--
            DEBUG OFF in tests, which is the opposite of the usual Symfony advice and deliberate here. With debug
            on, the framework logger writes every dispatched event to stdout; PHPUnit treats a test that produces
            output as RISKY, so all nine functional tests passed while the suite reported "OK, but there were
            issues!" — a green run with a warning nobody reads is the shape this project refuses.

            Nothing is lost: the functional suite asserts WIRE behaviour (status codes, JSON keys), every
            assertion message here already embeds the response body, and debug=0 is closer to what production
            actually does — so an error rendering differently under debug cannot hide behind a passing test.
        -->
        <env name="APP_DEBUG" value="0"/>
        <env name="APP_SECRET" value="test-secret-not-used-for-anything-signed"/>
        <env name="KERNEL_CLASS" value="Twes\Kernel"/>
        <!--
            The functional suite hits `/health/ready`, which asks the database three questions, so it points at the
            same throwaway credentials as the integration suite. A functional run that silently could not reach a
            database would report the readiness endpoint healthy for the wrong reason.
        -->
        <env name="DATABASE_URL" value="postgresql://twes:twes@127.0.0.1:5432/twes_in?serverVersion=18&amp;charset=utf8"/>
        <env name="DATABASE_URL_OWNER" value="postgresql://twes_owner:twes_owner@127.0.0.1:5432/twes_in?serverVersion=18&amp;charset=utf8"/>
        <env name="TWES_TEST_DB_USER" value="twes"/>
        <env name="TWES_TEST_DB_PASSWORD" value="twes"/>
        <env name="TWES_TEST_DB_OWNER_USER" value="twes_owner"/>
        <env name="TWES_TEST_DB_OWNER_PASSWORD" value="twes_owner"/>
        <env name="TWES_TEST_DB_BYPASS_USER" value="twes_bypass"/>
        <env name="TWES_TEST_DB_BYPASS_PASSWORD" value="twes_bypass"/>
        <env name="TWES_TEST_DB_MEMBER_USER" value="twes_member"/>
        <env name="TWES_TEST_DB_MEMBER_PASSWORD" value="twes_member"/>
        <env name="TWES_TEST_DB_REPLICATOR_USER" value="twes_replicator"/>
        <env name="TWES_TEST_DB_REPLICATOR_PASSWORD" value="twes_replicator"/>
        <env name="TWES_TEST_DB_TRUNCATOR_ROLE" value="twes_truncator"/>
        <env name="TWES_TEST_DB_PROBE_OWNER_ROLE" value="twes_probe_owner"/>
        <!--
            The EIGHTH role, and the only one under which pg_has_role(..., 'MEMBER') and
            pg_has_role(..., 'SET') disagree: granted to the runtime role WITH INHERIT FALSE, SET
            FALSE, so it is held but unreachable. Without it, reverting roleCanBeAssumedSql()'s
            narrow 'SET' mode to 'MEMBER' left the whole suite green — round 14.
        -->
        <env name="TWES_TEST_DB_UNSETTABLE_ROLE" value="twes_unsettable"/>
        <!--
            THE THREE CHAIN / INHERIT-ONLY PROBE ROLES, honoured by provision-test-database.sh and
            ABSENT FROM THIS FILE until round 21 — which matters more than a missing default, because
            CLAUDE.md names this file as THE TALLY to derive a CI's variables from
            (`grep -o 'TWES_TEST_DB_[A-Z_]*' api/phpunit.xml`). So a CI provisioned exactly as
            documented omitted all three, and silently skipped the cases that pin the reachability
            fixes. That is the round-15 defect verbatim: the set grew and the named tally did not.

            chain_inner / chain_outer form a TWO-LINK chain (INHERIT TRUE, SET FALSE then INHERIT
            FALSE, SET TRUE), which is what proves the SET-closure of USAGE is transitive rather than
            one level deep. inherit_only is granted INHERIT TRUE, SET FALSE.
        -->
        <env name="TWES_TEST_DB_CHAIN_INNER_ROLE" value="twes_chain_inner"/>
        <env name="TWES_TEST_DB_CHAIN_OUTER_ROLE" value="twes_chain_outer"/>
        <env name="TWES_TEST_DB_INHERIT_ONLY_ROLE" value="twes_inherit_only"/>
        <!--
            The LAST role created, and the only one whose name is not all-lowercase. (It said "the NINTH" until round 22: the three chain roles above were added AFTER it, so the ordinal was right when written and this file is the one that added them without fixing it. No ordinal is written now; derive the order from provision-test-database.sh.) `current_user::regrole`
            downcases, so the cast raised `role "twesmixedcase" does not exist` and the object check raised
            instead of answering. Round 13 replaced the cast; round 14 found the fix revertible with the suite
            green, because no other role can express the shape.
        -->
        <env name="TWES_TEST_DB_MIXED_CASE_ROLE" value="twesMixedCase"/>
        <env name="TWES_TEST_DB_MIXED_CASE_PASSWORD" value="twesMixedCase"/>
        <!--
            REQUIRED. Many tests build privileged fixtures with it. Derive the count with
            grep -c 'self::superuserConnection()', and four are the only proof that a
            security fix is load-bearing (the 'SET'-vs-'MEMBER' and pg_roles-vs-regrole mutants, and round
            15's rule and event-trigger carriers). Absent or wrong =&gt; the suite FAILS, not skips.
        -->
        <env name="TWES_TEST_DB_SUPERUSER" value="postgres"/>
        <env name="TWES_TEST_DB_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD" value="postgres"/>
    </php>
</phpunit>
